\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
A More Secure System for Transmitting
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

OSC Performance Data over UDP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Paul V.
 Miller
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Open Sound Control"
target "https://ccrma.stanford.edu/groups/osc/index.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (OSC) is a widely used protocol for sending control data over networks in live performance and interactive art.
 It is a relatively simple protocol typically sent over UDP that maintains the low latency necessary for audio applications.
 However,
 OSC messages are frequently transmitted over shared WiFi networks without any authentication or integrity protection.
 Because UDP provides no built-in authentication or session management,
 any device on the same network can inject control messages if it knows (or discovers) the destination IP and port.
 In a live performance,
 this can result in a catastrophic attack.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The purpose of this project is to add some basic security layers to OSC.
 Although OSC is a general protocol that can be implemented by servers,
 microcontrollers,
 or other devices,
 I will be working in the very common Max/MSP environment.
 OSC externals for Max can be downloaded from the Center for New Music and Audio Technologies (CNMAT) at UC-Berkeley 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "https://cnmat.berkeley.edu/downloads"
target "here"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Risk Assessment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a typical performance or installation that utilizes OSC over WiFi systems,
 an attacker has to be on the same network as the victim.
 For open networks,
 gaining this kind of access is trivial.
 For closed networks,
 it is not easy to crack a router's WiFi password (particularly if it is encoded using WPA2 or WPA3).
 However,
 musicians and artists are frequently unaware of the security vulnerabilities that routers expose.
 For example,
 routers are often shared assets that pass fluidly from person to person,
 with several individuals aware of the password.
 Router passwords are frequently shared in a cooperative environment.
 The sticker on the router advertising the credentials may not be removed,
 boldly advertising the password to any 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

curious
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 passerby who snaps a photo of the tech setup.
 Consequently,
 an attack would most likely come from an insider wanting to disrupt a performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the router password is known,
 an attacker would still need to know the port number through which OSC data was being transmitted.
 In TCP and UDP,
 port numbers are 16 bits long.
 Certain ports are more frequently used for OSC transmission because of their prevalence in online tutorials:
 for example,
 most Max/MSP tutorials use ports 7000,
 8000 or 9000.
 SuperCollider defaults to port 57120.
 Although port scanning is easy for TCP services discovery,
 it is more difficult on UDP.
 Although UDP services may not respond to invalid probes,
 it remains feasible to scan all 65,536 ports in a short period of time;
 a single successful OSC injection would tell the attacker that they found the right port.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Distinct from but related to message injection,
 
\shape italic
flooding
\shape default
 is a second problem that frequently occurs even when legitimately sending OSC control codes.
 Too many messages arriving at the same time can overload the ability to process them all,
 causing sequencing and timing issues in audio applications.
 Message flooding could occur if an attacker captured encrypted OSC control codes and replayed them,
 overwhelming the victim.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are no native security features for sending OSC messages over UDP apart from the general features of WiFi routers.
 The protocol operates entirely on a trust model.
 Most artists using these systems are not trained in security measures.
 I have personally witnessed radically insecure setups at electronic music performances.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Breach of this trust model can easily result in a catastrophic attack.
 Injecting OSC messages or flooding the system can grind a performance completely to a halt.
 If the unfortunate performers were to restart,
 the security issues would be unresolved and the attack could resume.
 The impact on a live performance could be enormous,
 potentially resulting in disruption,
 significant reputational harm,
 and audience dissatisfaction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary:
 a 
\shape italic
very realistic threat
\shape default
 could come from an attacker who already has another device on the WiFi router,
 and knows the port.
 A 
\shape italic
moderately realistic threat
\shape default
 could come from someone who is on the WiFi router but brute-forces the port number.
 A 
\shape italic
less realistic threat
\shape default
 would be an external attacker who cracks WiFi,
 then scans ports.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Goals and Scope
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary goal of this project is to design and evaluate a lightweight,
 application-layer security mechanism.
 The system must be simple enough to ensure low latency,
 which is critical to audio applications.
 Specifically,
 I intend to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Demonstrate the vulnerability of unauthenticated OSC control streams to attacks on a shared network;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Design a security envelope that provides:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Message authenticity and integrity (using HMAC – hash-based message authentication code),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Anti-replay protection (using sequence numbers),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Basic rate limiting and validation.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Implement a verification gateway on the receiving host that enforces these protections,
 while retaining compatibility with existing Max/MSP or Pure Data (Pd) patches;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Evaluate the overhead and practical impact of these protections in a real-time performance context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The scope of the project focuses on application-layer protections,
 and does not attempt to modify UDP,
 WiFi or the OSC specification itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
System Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The baseline system consists of a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

victim
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Raspberry Pi Pico W,
 transmitting OSC messages over UDP to a laptop,
 and an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

attacker
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Pico W capable of sending arbitrary messages to the same IP and port.
 A laptop receives these packets and uses the control data in a typical way,
 to affect some aspect of live audio processing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The proposed secure design includes a verification gateway on the receiving laptop.
 Instead of listening directly to a WiFi-facing port,
 we will receive OSC from 
\family typewriter
127.0.0.1
\family default
.
 This gateway will only forward verified OSC payloads.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each UDP datagram will contain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A 12-byte header that contains a version number (1 byte),
 a reserved field for future extensibility (1 byte),
 sender device ID (4 bytes),
 a monotonic sequence number (4 bytes),
 and a payload length (2 bytes);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The OSC payload (unmodified);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A 16-byte HMAC authentication tag (e.g.,
 HMAC-SHA256,
 truncated to 16 bytes).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The HMAC is a keyed hash function that is computed over the header and OSC payload using a pre-shared symmetric key,
 provisioned 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

out-of-band
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 pre-configured on both devices and never transmitted over the network).
 This general type of system is very common and used extensively in web security (GitHub),
 cloud infrastructure,
 and even SSH.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This approach should yield significant benefits,
 including:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Authenticity and integrity:
 only devices possessing the shared key can generate valid messages;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Anti-replay:
 sequence numbers will prevent re-sending of captured valid packets;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Basic availability protections:
 Rate limiting and message validation prevent flooding and malformed input attacks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Evaluation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I will make short videos of a baseline demonstration simulating an attack;
 a replay attack;
 and then a secure system demonstration.
 To benchmark the system,
 I will measure end-to-end latency since this is one of the most critical aspects in live audio systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Expected Outcomes and Further Work
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By the end of the project,
 the system will demonstrate the practical insecurity of unauthenticated OSC over WiFi.
 I will provide a working prototype of a more secure OSC-style control channel,
 and show that a lightweight,
 application layer authentication can effectively prevent injection attacks without significant latency overhead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Further work beyond the scope of this class project could include integrating this kind of a system into standalone Pd and Max/MSP objects,
 a key provisioning and rotation mechanism,
 potential encryption,
 and support for multiple trusted senders.
\end_layout

